        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJRCTIVES:<P>
<P>Many astronauts experience space sickness, first as their bodies adjust to the low-gravity environment of space, and then again, when they return to Earth. During this re-adaptation period, they may experience symptoms such as dizziness, nausea, and vomiting. They may also have difficulty maintaining balance in the absence of visual cues. The mechanisms underlying this re-adaptation process remain unclear and are the subject of this research project.<P>

APPROACH:<P>This experiment will examine the changes in the brain, especially shape and neurotransmitter activity, that are involved in sensory and motor adaptation. Five flight rats and five ground controls will undergo transcardial perfusion one day post-flight. After perfusion, the brain tissue will be harvested and shipped to the primary investigator, who will process it for electron microscopy and immunocytochemistry studies.<P>

RESULTS:<P>The payload was not recovered.
        </div>

